<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aurangzeb</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1658">1658</a>–<a href="/years/1707">1707</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aurangzeb was the sixth Mughal emperor, reigning from 1658 to 1707. He expanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> to its greatest territorial extent, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, and enforced a more orthodox Islamic policy, including reimposing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> tax on non-Muslims. His long wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and others exhausted the empire's resources and contributed to its decline after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurangzeb.jpg" alt="Aurangzeb" width="309" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cordanrad; derivative work by Rani nurmai · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurangzeb-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The massive walls of Bijapur, with great bastions mounting heavy guns such as the bronze Malik-i-Maidan, protected the capital, which resisted a Mughal army under Jai Singh in 1665–1666 before falling to <b>Aurangzeb</b> in 1686.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Hyderabad State grew out of the Mughal administration of the Deccan, the plateau of south-central India conquered by <b>Aurangzeb</b> in the 1680s, when the sultanates of Bijapur and Golconda fell.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The state survived <b>Aurangzeb</b>'s long Deccan war after Shivaji's death, and by the early 18th century the Mughals' overextension and the fracturing of the Deccan had opened the way for Maratha expansion across India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Aurangzeb</b> (r. 1658–1707), the empire reached its greatest territorial extent, annexing the Deccan sultanates of Bijapur in 1686 and Golconda in 1687 and waging decades of war against the Marathas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Raj Singh I (r. 1652–1680) opposed <b>Aurangzeb</b>’s reimposition of the jizya tax and supported the Rathores of Marwar in the Rajput war of 1679–1681, which ended in a compromise peace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The ninth Guru, Tegh Bahadur, was executed in Delhi in 1675 on the orders of the emperor <b>Aurangzeb</b>; Sikh tradition remembers him as giving his life to defend the religious freedom of Hindus being forced to convert.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
